 document ends with an explicit design-only statement.", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Does fusing Adam (TorchOpt) yield a less complex, more interpretable gradient graph in TorchViz output than unfused Adam?\n\n**Status: design-only deliverable. No experiment has been run; no results are reported or implied.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Objective\n\n**Question.** Does fusing operations within the Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt) result in a less complex and more interpretable gradient graph compared to the output from TorchViz?\n\n**Explicit interpretation (fixed for this design).** We are comparing **TorchViz's rendering of the unfused Adam step** versus **TorchViz's rendering of the fused Adam step** \u2014 i.e., the *same visualization tool* applied to two optimizer arms under an identical workload. This is *not* a TorchViz-vs-TorchOpt tool comparison, and not a forward/backward model-graph comparison: the object of study is the op graph of the **optimizer update step** (the computation that turns gradients + state into a parameter update), rendered by TorchViz.\n\n**Hypothesis (H1).** The fused-Adam step renders in TorchViz as a strictly less complex graph (fewer nodes/edges/op-types; shallower depth; fewer generic pointwise ops) and is scored as more interpretable (higher semantic-block coverage; higher blinded human readability) than the unfused-Adam step on identical inputs.\n\n**Decision rule (pre-registered).** H1 is supported if, across the shape sweep: (i) every structural complexity metric is \u2264 for fused and strictly < for fused on a majority of shapes; (ii) named-block coverage is complete for fused vs. fragmented for unfused; (iii) blinded human readability is higher for fused with inter-rater agreement \u2265 threshold (\u00a74.2). Otherwise the design reports H1 not supported, with the metric tables as evidence.\n\n## 2. Workload\n\n**Primary workload \u2014 pure optimizer micro-workload (no forward model).**\n- A fixed set of leaf parameter tensors